msgid "cengarde"
msgstr ""

msgid "Sends every packet of a WireGuard tunnel to a VPS over all the selected uplinks at once, and the first copy to arrive wins: one uplink failing or lagging no longer drops the connection. Setup: copy the cloud-config from the VPS tab when creating the VPS, then enter its IP here, pick the uplinks and enable."
msgstr ""

msgid "General"
msgstr ""

msgid "VPS"
msgstr ""

msgid "Tunnel"
msgstr ""

msgid "Advanced"
msgstr ""

msgid "Enabled"
msgstr ""

msgid "VPS addresses"
msgstr ""

msgid "Public IPs of the VPS as the provider shows them, IPv4 and IPv6, in order of preference: each uplink uses the first one of a family it has, and tries the next one when the VPS does not answer there. A name works too: the router looks it up through each uplink (the carrier's DNS first), never through the tunnel, and follows it when its IP changes (dynamic DNS). An MTU above 1396 does not fit if the name has an IPv6 address that the uplinks use."
msgstr ""

msgid "At most %d addresses"
msgstr ""

msgid "VPS port"
msgstr ""

msgid "UDP port of cengarde on the VPS."
msgstr ""

msgid "Uplinks"
msgstr ""

msgid "The interfaces to bond: one per modem or VLAN, each with its own default route. cengarde gives them a metric of their own if they share one and puts them in the wan firewall zone."
msgstr ""

msgid "Pairing secret"
msgstr ""

msgid "Every key of the tunnel, and its address unless you set one, derives from it: the VPS only needs this value, which goes in its cloud-config. A new secret means updating the VPS too."
msgstr ""

msgid "Expected the output of \"cengarde genkey\" (44 characters)"
msgstr ""

msgid "A VPS set up with the current secret will stop working until it gets the new one. Generate a new secret?"
msgstr ""

msgid "Generate"
msgstr ""

msgid "Name on the VPS"
msgstr ""

msgid "How a VPS that serves several routers calls this one: 1 to 12 characters of a-z, 0-9 and -, not starting with - and not \"default\". Each router of a VPS needs its own. Empty: router."
msgstr ""

msgid "1 to 12 characters of a-z, 0-9 and -, not starting with - and not \"default\""
msgstr ""

msgid "Address in the tunnel"
msgstr ""

msgid "The address of this router inside the tunnel, 10.79.X.Y from 10.79.0.2 to 10.79.255.254; each router of a VPS needs its own. Empty: the one derived from the saved secret, which the field shows in grey (after a new secret, save and apply to see it). A router installed before this version keeps 10.79.0.2: changing it means telling the VPS too."
msgstr ""

msgid "Expected 10.79.X.Y, from 10.79.0.2 to 10.79.255.254"
msgstr ""

msgid "VPS cloud-config"
msgstr ""

msgid "For a new VPS. Paste it as \"user data\" (cloud-init) when creating an Ubuntu 22.04 or 24.04 VPS, e.g. on Vultr. It builds the same cengarde version as this router and sets up WireGuard, NAT and IP pass, with the name and address of this router. Setup log on the VPS: /var/log/cloud-init-output.log."
msgstr ""

msgid "Copy"
msgstr ""

msgid "Download"
msgstr ""

msgid "This router on a VPS that already serves others"
msgstr ""

msgid "Instead of the cloud-config, run this on the VPS over SSH: it adds this router next to the others, with its own name and address in the tunnel. When it asks, paste the pairing secret shown above: it is never part of the command."
msgstr ""

msgid "The command shows once the name and the address in the tunnel are valid and known: after a new secret, save and apply first."
msgstr ""

msgid "Manage the WireGuard tunnel"
msgstr ""

msgid "Creates the interface wgcg with the keys derived from the secret and this router's address in the tunnel (VPS tab), in the wan firewall zone. Off: set up WireGuard yourself, cengarde only listens on 127.0.0.1 at the VPS port."
msgstr ""

msgid "Tunnel mode"
msgstr ""

msgid "WireGuard: the tunnel goes through the interface wgcg, encrypted. Native (experimental): no WireGuard, cengarde carries the packets itself through the interface cg0, with the same addresses and routes; it is authenticated (nobody can inject or replay packets) but NOT encrypted, so the carriers see the traffic. The VPS has to be in the same mode, for every router it serves: \"sudo cengarde-vps-setup tunnel native\" (or wireguard to go back). A change restarts the engine."
msgstr ""

msgid "WireGuard"
msgstr ""

msgid "Native (experimental, not encrypted)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Route all traffic through the tunnel"
msgstr ""

msgid "Through routes more specific than the default ones, which the uplinks keep for cengarde itself."
msgstr ""

msgid "IPv6 through the tunnel"
msgstr ""

msgid "Carries IPv6 through the tunnel too, translated (NAT66) here and at the VPS: this router gets a ULA address in the tunnel, derived from the secret, and with \"Route all traffic through the tunnel\" the IPv6 of the router and of the LAN leaves through the VPS. The VPS needs IPv6 of its own and the command \"sudo cengarde-vps-setup ipv6 on\" (the cloud-config of a new VPS follows this switch when all traffic goes through the tunnel). The LAN needs a ULA prefix (Network > Interfaces > Global network options); its devices have only that, so they prefer IPv4 for sites that have both, and this serves mainly IPv6-only sites. Off until it has been tested on real hardware."
msgstr ""

msgid "IP pass"
msgstr ""

msgid "The VPS forwards TCP and UDP ports 1024-65000 to this router and UPnP hands them on to the LAN: devices and services here get the public IP of the VPS. The VPS follows this switch on its own, within a second of saving."
msgstr ""

msgid "Needs miniupnpd-nftables (and luci-app-upnp to see the mappings)."
msgstr ""

msgid "STUN server"
msgstr ""

msgid "Only for IP pass when no VPS address is IPv4: UPnP then learns the public IPv4 of the VPS from this STUN server, through the tunnel (e.g. stun.cloudflare.com). Empty: UPnP announces no public IP and hands no ports on to the LAN."
msgstr ""

msgid "STUN port"
msgstr ""

msgid "DNS servers"
msgstr ""

msgid "Used through the tunnel; the uplinks stop announcing their carriers' DNS servers, which usually refuse queries coming from the VPS."
msgstr ""

msgid "IPv6 servers are left out while IPv6 does not go through the tunnel: the router would reach them around it. Without an IPv4 server, 1.1.1.1 and 9.9.9.9 are used."
msgstr ""

msgid "Tunnel MTU"
msgstr ""

msgid "In a 1500-byte path, packets of up to 1416 bytes fit over IPv4 and of up to 1396 over IPv6. Larger ones get fragmented, and mobile networks often drop the fragments."
msgstr ""

msgid "At most %d with an IPv6 VPS address"
msgstr ""

msgid "IPv6 on the uplinks"
msgstr ""

msgid "Adds a DHCPv6 interface on top of each DHCP or static uplink (named after it, ending in 6), so that cengarde can reach the VPS over the IPv6 of the modems. Their IPv6 prefixes never reach the LAN."
msgstr ""

msgid "Next VPS address after (ms)"
msgstr ""

msgid "An uplink that gets no answer from the VPS for this long tries the next VPS address it can use, IPv4 or IPv6, in the list's order. 0: never."
msgstr ""

msgid "0 (never) or at least %d ms with this probe interval"
msgstr ""

msgid "Standby after falling behind (ms)"
msgstr ""

msgid "An uplink that stays this far behind the fastest one in a direction for the settle time goes on standby that way (probes only) until it catches up; the other direction is decided apart. 0: never."
msgstr ""

msgid "Settle time (ms)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Minimum active links"
msgstr ""

msgid "No uplink goes on standby if that would leave fewer than this many in use."
msgstr ""

msgid "Reorder at the receiver"
msgstr ""

msgid "The end that receives holds a packet that arrives ahead of a missing one until the missing one comes or its time runs out, and hands them to WireGuard in order. The VPS does the same with what it receives. While every packet is copied over all the uplinks nothing is held, so it changes nothing and costs almost nothing; it is what makes the split below usable."
msgstr ""

msgid "Longest wait for a missing packet (ms)"
msgstr ""

msgid "How long a packet that arrived early waits for the missing one before it is handed on anyway. It waits less when the slowest uplink would have delivered the missing one by then. A longer wait copes with slower uplinks, and adds delay when a packet is really lost."
msgstr ""

msgid "Packets never held, up to (bytes)"
msgstr ""

msgid "WireGuard packets of up to this size (keepalives, empty TCP ACKs) are handed on at once, and so are the handshakes. 0: only the handshakes. A larger value lets small data packets overtake the big ones that are held, which TCP and QUIC can take for losses."
msgstr ""

msgid "Split large packets across the uplinks (experimental)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Instead of a copy over every uplink, each large packet (over 256 bytes, by default) goes over one uplink, chosen by weight; the small ones and the handshakes still go over all of them. This adds up the speed of the uplinks (in the lab, two equal uplinks carried 1.98 times what one does), but it protects less than copying: an uplink that dies keeps its share of the packets until the engine takes it out (about 300 ms by estimate, 1.5 s before that), and the packets sent to it in the meantime are lost; the slowest uplink in the split sets the latency of all (see the largest delay difference below); and a weight above what an uplink can carry fills the queue of its modem and makes the uplink unstable. The VPS follows this router: it splits the download the same way, with the same weights, when the router asks for it, and needs no setting. It needs \"Reorder at the receiver\"."
msgstr ""

msgid "Weight of each uplink in the split"
msgstr ""

msgid "One entry per uplink, as NAME=WEIGHT (for example wan=3), with NAME as in the list of uplinks of the General tab and a weight from 0 to 1000. An uplink gets a share of the split packets in proportion to its weight; with no entry its weight is 1. 0: the uplink carries a copy of every packet, split or not. The weights do not follow the speed of the uplinks: choose them in proportion to what each one carries."
msgstr ""

msgid "Largest delay difference to split (ms)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Only the uplinks whose delay towards the VPS is at most this much behind the fastest one get a share of the split; the others leave it (the next option says what they carry), and get their share back once they stay within three quarters of it for the settle time. The VPS does the same with the download, with its own setting (30 ms unless changed there). Measured in the lab: two equal uplinks split give almost twice one uplink, but 50 ms apart most of that gain is lost, and 150 ms apart the speed collapses: the slowest uplink in the split sets the pace of all. 0: no limit."
msgstr ""

msgid "Copies to the uplinks out of the split"
msgstr ""

msgid "On (as up to version 0.6.2): an uplink that leaves the split for its delay carries a copy of every packet, to fill the gaps of the others, and with fewer than two uplinks left in the split everything goes on every uplink. Off: it carries only the small packets and the handshakes, which go on every uplink, and one uplink left in the split carries everything that is split. An uplink falls behind most often because its modem's queue is full: in the first field test the copies loaded the late uplinks even more, and the router dropped thousands of packets; in the lab they helped QUIC. Only for the upload: the VPS has its own setting (on unless changed there)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Probe interval (ms)"
msgstr ""

msgid "While there is traffic; each idle link is probed every second, or at this interval if it is longer. The next VPS address is tried after three idle probes at the least."
msgstr ""

msgid "Busy polling (µs)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Keeps polling this long after traffic instead of sleeping: lower latency, more CPU. 0: off."
msgstr ""

msgid "Threads per uplink"
msgstr ""

msgid "Off runs the code of every release so far. On gives each uplink a thread of its own, so that a stalled uplink or thread no longer holds back the others; it takes more CPU at low traffic, and a stall of the main thread, which checks every packet, still holds back every uplink at once. One thread runs the new structure without its threads, to tell a problem of the threads from one of the new code. A change restarts the engine."
msgstr ""

